## Propagating Trace Context Across RabbitMQ

RabbitMQ doesn't automatically carry W3C `traceparent`/`tracestate` headers. You need to manually inject/extract them using `System.Diagnostics`.

### Publisher (Service A)

```csharp
using System.Diagnostics;

// Define an ActivitySource for your producer
static readonly ActivitySource Source = new("ServiceA.Messaging");

// When publishing:
using var activity = Source.StartActivity("OrderEvent Publish", ActivityKind.Producer);

var props = channel.CreateBasicProperties();
props.Headers ??= new Dictionary<string, object>();

// Inject current trace context into message headers
if (Activity.Current != null)
{
    props.Headers["traceparent"] = Activity.Current.Id!;
    if (Activity.Current.TraceStateString is { } ts)
        props.Headers["tracestate"] = ts;
}

channel.BasicPublish(exchange, routingKey, props, body);
```

### Consumer (Service B)

```csharp
static readonly ActivitySource Source = new("ServiceB.Messaging");

// In your consumer callback:
var headers = ea.BasicProperties.Headers;
string? traceparent = headers?.ContainsKey("traceparent") == true
    ? Encoding.UTF8.GetString((byte[])headers["traceparent"])
    : null;
string? tracestate = headers?.ContainsKey("tracestate") == true
    ? Encoding.UTF8.GetString((byte[])headers["tracestate"])
    : null;

// Re-hydrate the parent context
ActivityContext parentCtx = default;
if (traceparent != null &&
    ActivityContext.TryParse(traceparent, tracestate, out var parsed))
{
    parentCtx = parsed;
}

using var activity = Source.StartActivity(
    "OrderEvent Process",
    ActivityKind.Consumer,
    parentCtx          // ← links this span to the producer's trace
);

// … handle the message
```

### Wire it up in `Program.cs` (both services)

```csharp
builder.Services.AddOpenTelemetry()
    .WithTracing(t => t
        .AddSource("ServiceA.Messaging")   // or ServiceB.Messaging
        .AddAspNetCoreInstrumentation()
        .AddOtlpExporter());
```

### Why this works

| Concept | Detail |
|---|---|
| **`traceparent`** | Carries the trace ID + parent span ID across the queue |
| **`ActivityKind.Producer/Consumer`** | Tells your trace backend these are async messaging spans |
| **`parentCtx`** | Restores the causal link so the consumer span appears under the same trace |

The key insight: RabbitMQ headers are `byte[]` on read, so you must `Encoding.UTF8.GetString()` when extracting. If you're using MassTransit or NServiceBus, they do this propagation automatically.
